  <book xml:id="mathematics-book">
    <title>Irish School Maths</title>
    <subtitle>Making Maths as Simple as Possible</subtitle>
    <frontmatter>
      <bibinfo>
        <author>
          <personname>Ronan Downes</personname>
        </author>
      </bibinfo>
      <titlepage>
        <titlepage-items/>
      </titlepage>

      <preface xml:id="welcome">
        <title>Welcome</title>
        <p>Welcome to <em>Irish School Maths</em>, a free, interactive mathematics book for students from First Year to Sixth Year, whatever course or level you are studying.</p>
        <p><em>My promise is to support and challenge you, whatever your starting point.</em> Mathematics should be something you can read, see, explore and enjoy. You'll find clear explanations, carefully designed diagrams, worked examples, dynamic graphs, interactive applications and quizzes. Where available, audio and video offer further ways in. Instead of simply looking at mathematics on a page, I want you to experiment with it, change things, make predictions and receive useful feedback as you go.</p>
        <p>Mathematics builds on earlier ideas. Sometimes the difficulty isn't today's topic but something you met years ago. Diagnostic questions and links to prerequisite material are intended to help you find what you need without getting stuck. And if you're ready to go further, follow your curiosity: try a harder investigation, a project or even a glimpse of Leaving Certificate mathematics while you're still in Junior Cycle.</p>
        <p>Mathematics is more than calculations. Its language, logic, diagrams and connections matter too. One of my greatest pleasures after years of teaching is staying with an idea until something difficult becomes wonderfully simple. <em>Don't settle for a high mark if there's still something you want to understand.</em></p>
        <p>You might think I'm crazy putting an equation in a welcome. But take a look:</p>
        <me>\frac{1}{1^2}+\frac{1}{2^2}+\frac{1}{3^2}+\frac{1}{4^2}+\cdots=\frac{\pi^2}{6}</me>
        <p>Infinitely many fractions involving square numbers add up to an expression involving <m>\pi</m> — the number you know from circles. <em>OMG!</em> Leonhard Euler proved this beautiful connection. You don't need to understand it yet. I hope one day you'll look at it and feel the same excitement I do.</p>
        <p><em>Come with me. Let's discover the craft of mathematics.</em></p>
        <p><em>Ronan Downes</em></p>
      </preface>

      <preface xml:id="project-preface">
        <title>To the Teacher</title>
        <p><em>Irish School Maths</em> is free, openly developed and very much a work in progress. There's still plenty to add and improve, and you may find some topics incomplete.</p>
        <p>I'd love to hear what you think — what works, what doesn't, or what you would like to see included. Please feel free to share the book, its interactive activities and quizzes with your students. I hope it helps them explore mathematics and find their own way through it.</p>
        <p><em>Ronan Downes</em></p>
      </preface>

      <preface xml:id="about-the-author">
        <title>About the Author</title>
        <p>Ronan Downes is a Mathematics teacher based in Limerick, Ireland. He studied Physics at NUI Galway and later graduated with an honours degree in Physical Optoelectronics from the University of Essex. After completing a Higher Diploma in Education, he taught Physics, Mathematics and Science in secondary schools for four years.</p>
        <p>He then moved into telecommunications, working as a systems engineer and access engineer with Esat Digifone. He later became self-employed in multimedia, including photography, video production and web development.</p>
        <p>A lifelong interest in learning brought him back to university, where he completed a <url href="https://www.ul.ie/study/postgraduate/mathematical-modelling-msc" visual="Master's degree in Mathematical Modelling"/> at the University of Limerick. He subsequently returned to classroom teaching and later completed a <url href="https://www.cct.ie/course/masters-data-analytics-postgraduate-msc/" visual="Master's degree in Data Analytics"/> at CCT College Dublin. His experience in modelling, programming and computational thinking informs the explanations and interactive resources in this book.</p>
        <p>He has also worked for many years as an examiner with the State Examinations Commission. That experience has reinforced the importance of teaching students to communicate their mathematical thinking.</p>
        <p>His aim is to reduce unnecessary cognitive load without removing the ideas, reasoning and choices that give mathematics its meaning. His guiding principle is inspired by a saying commonly attributed to Albert Einstein: <em>Making maths as simple as possible, but not too simple.</em></p>
      </preface>
    </frontmatter>

